{"t":1788349884,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"d91ea17f-aa74-4514-aec9-83cc18980333","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-02T08:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 020800\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n400 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHeat Advisories have been issued for the southern half of the \nforecast area. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Daily severe weather threat through the end of the week.\n\n- 2) Hot and humid conditions yield likely record high minimum \n     temperatures this week.\n\n- 3) Heat & thunderstorms begin to decrease heading into \n     Labor Day weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily severe weather threat through the end of \nthe week.\n\nMultiple rounds of thunderstorms, some likely severe with\ndamaging winds, are likely today through Friday as upper level\ndisturbances move from the Great Lks into the Mid Atlantic\nstates. Already seen a small cluster of thunderstorms over south\ncentral Pennsylvania that will move through the area later this\nmorning. Two additional rounds of thunderstorms seem likely\ntoday, one this afternoon, and a second one late this evening \ninto the overnight hours tonight into Thursday. Thunderstorms \nwill ride southeast along and north of a backdoor front \ncurrently pushing south through northern VA and southern MD as \nseen on TDWRs and wind shifts to the NE at IAD and DCA a couple \nof hours ago. The severe threat will not be necessarily tied to\nthe afternoon and evening hours as record warm nighttime \ntemperatures and high humidity yield strong instability and \nkeep thunderstorms going strong well into the overnight hours. \n\nBy Friday, cold front should have cleared much of the forecast \narea causing a stabilizing effect limiting the risk of severe\nweather, but showers and thunderstorms will still be possible\nnorth of the sfc front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions yield likely record \nhigh minimum temperatures this week.\n\nDecent model agreement that areas south of US-33 and US-211 will\nsee heat indices meet heat advisory criteria this afternoon.\nBased on yesterday's high temperature readings and greater\nconfidence, issued Heat Advisories for the southern half of the\nfcst area. \n\nThursday's max temperature forecast has a lot more \nuncertainty due to potential for more convection. If convection\ndoesn't materialize, more record high minimums are possible\nagain today, but that doesn't seem likely at this time, except\nonly at Charlottesville. \n\nRecord high minimum temperatures were set yesterday at IAD, DCA,\nand HGR. RERs were issued earlier. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Heat & thunderstorms begin to decrease heading into \nLabor Day weekend.\n\nHigh pressure tracks southeast from the Great Lakes on Saturday, \npushing a cold front further south of the region. This should \nbring a drop in heat & humidity across the Mid-Atlantic, with \nafternoon highs ranging in the mid-80s on Saturday. As mid-level dry \nair continues to track across the area, temperatures should continue \nto decrease as the weekened goes on. By the Labor Day holiday, it \nshould feel relatively mild & dry, with dewpoints ranging in the \nupper-50s across the region. \n\nThunderstorm chances also begin to decrease past Friday once the \ncold front tracks to the south. A few showers & thunderstorms remain \npossible along the Blue Ridge as low-level moisture persists \nSaturday evening, but overall conditions should gradually clear as \nthe weekend progresses. Surface high pressure tracks overhead by \nMonday, further reducing precipitation chances over the holiday. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n\nMultiple rounds of thunderstorms are expected today through\nFriday. Given low confidence on timing, issued long PROB30\ngroups for now. Some low cigs are expected at BWI and MTN later\nthis morning. \n\nPrevailing VFR conditions are anticipated at terminals throughout \nthe weekend. A couple of thunderstorms remain possible in the \nvicinity of KCHO through Saturday afternoon & evening. Light \nnorthwesterly winds shift easterly by Sunday evening. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds 10kt or less outside of thunderstorms. SMWs will likely be\nrequired at some point this afternoon and tonight and again\nThursday.\n\nWind gusts stay below 15 knots Saturday night and Sunday, with \nnortherly winds shifting northeast by Sunday. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\n\nRecord high minimums were set yesterday at IAD, DCA, and HGR.\nPlease RERs for detail info. More record high minimums are\npossible again today, mainly at CHO where convection is less\nlikely.\n\nBelow is a list of record warm temperatures for September 2 and\n3.\n\n(A '+' indicates the record also occurred in previous years).\n\nThe period of record for most sites dates back to the late 1800s, \nexcept for Sterling-Dulles Airport which dates back to 1960.\n\n====================================================================\n                            SEPTEMBER 2\n====================================================================\nSite                      Record Daily High   Record Daily Warm Low\nBaltimore (BWI) MD           99 (1953)            75 (1932+)\nWashington DC               101 (1980)            78 (1980)\nSterling-Dulles Airport VA   99 (1980)            72 (2020+)\nBaltimore (Downtown) MD      98 (1980+)           80 (1980)\nAnnapolis MD                 97 (1998)            79 (2018+)\nHagerstown MD               100 (1953)            73 (2018+)\nMartinsburg WV              103 (1932)            72 (2000+)\nCharlottesville VA          105 (1953)            80 (1930)\n\n====================================================================\n                            SEPTEMBER 3\n====================================================================\nSite                      Record Daily High   Record Daily Warm Low\nBaltimore (BWI) MD           98 (2023)            79 (1993)\nWashington DC                98 (1953)            79 (1993)\nSterling-Dulles Airport VA   99 (2023)            77 (1993)\nBaltimore (Downtown) MD      98 (2023)            82 (1993)\nAnnapolis MD                 99 (1993)            79 (2018)\nHagerstown MD               100 (1953)            72 (2018+)\nMartinsburg WV              102 (1953)            71 (1957)\nCharlottesville VA          104 (1953)            75 (1930)\n\n\n\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...Heat Advisory from 11 AM this morning to 8 PM EDT this evening \n     for VAZ025>027-029-036>040-050-051-056-504.\nWV...Heat Advisory from 11 AM this morning to 8 PM EDT this evening \n     for WVZ055-502-506.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/SRT\nAVIATION...LFR/SRT\nMARINE...LFR/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1788349856,"days":{"2026-09-02":{"hi":88,"lo":75,"pop":70,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-03":{"hi":93,"lo":77,"pop":40,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-04":{"hi":92,"lo":72,"pop":60,"short":"Slight Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-05":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":9,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-06":{"hi":82,"lo":64,"pop":7,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-07":{"hi":83,"lo":66,"pop":10,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-08":{"hi":85,"lo":66,"pop":13,"short":"Partly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1788346800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,13300,null,null,8400,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,9100,11000,10000,4700,10000,10000,10000,10000,10000,11000,11000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[8.3,9.7,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.5,9.3,9.3,8.8,8.8,8.8,6.9,6.9,6.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[4,4,5,6,6,6,5,5